quantizr-pixels: a program around the quantizr library, reading raw pixels and writing the palette and the indices.

Upstream:   https://github.com/DarthSim/quantizr (MIT, Copyright (c) 2017 Sergey Alexandrovich; the licence text is ./LICENSE, copied from the
            tagged commit, SHA-256 407b2d167f73ef78da1fcbfb6c2f1051f195b1984b9bfeeac0a39b34c198a7fe)
Tag:        v1.4.3
Commit:     cfb26aaf3039ac1179d42a66cc7988c8c6feeba9
Package:    quantizr 1.4.3 on crates.io, checksum b28ab4659f6754222a9e96c7a48f68dba03a620f23803c67ccd4ca7c35d55f5f (Cargo.lock). Its src/ directory
            and Cargo.toml are byte for byte those of the tagged commit (compared when the program was added).

Copied from upstream (upstream path -> path here):
  LICENSE                          -> LICENSE        unchanged

Followed, not copied:
  example/src/main.rs              -> src/main.rs    the calls of the library (Options, set_max_colors, QuantizeResult::quantize,
      set_dithering_level, remap_image, get_palette) are the example's, in its order. Everything else is ours: the input (the width and the
      height as two little-endian u32, then RGBA bytes, on standard input, bounded by MAX_PIXELS), the output (the palette and the indices
      on standard output) and the argument parser. The example reads and writes PNG files through the png crate, which this program does not use.

The library is not copied: quantizr comes from crates.io at the version and checksum of Cargo.lock, and the program has no other dependency.
The program is built by scripts/install-quantizr-pixels.sh with the pinned Rust toolchain, for the musl target.
Additions of ours: [profile.release] panic = "abort", lto = "thin" and codegen-units = 1 in Cargo.toml (3 to 4 percent of the run on a 768 x 512
picture, linux/arm64; the palette and the indices are the same bytes).

Cargo.lock                         ours: written by cargo for the one crate; the checksum is crates.io's.
